      body: `<p><strong>Performance anxiety is the single most common cause of ED in young men, and it works through a cruel feedback loop that turns one bad night into a pattern.</strong> Understanding the mechanism is the first step to breaking it.</p><p>An erection requires the body's "rest and relax" (parasympathetic) nervous system to be in charge - that's what lets penile arteries widen and fill. Anxiety does the opposite: it triggers the "fight or flight" (sympathetic) response, flooding the body with adrenaline, which constricts those same arteries and actively works <em>against</em> an erection. In other words, worrying about getting an erection produces exactly the physiological state that prevents one.</p><p>Here's the loop: a man has an off night (for any reason - alcohol, tiredness, nerves). He interprets it as a sign something's wrong. Next time, he's watching himself anxiously, "spectating" instead of being present. The anxiety triggers the adrenaline response, the erection fails, which "confirms" his fear - and now the anxiety is worse for next time. The erection was never the problem; the fear of losing it was. This is why young, physically healthy men can develop persistent ED with perfectly good arteries. The good news is that because the cause is a cycle, breaking the cycle - through reassurance, reducing the pressure, sometimes a short course of ED medication to restore confidence, and addressing the underlying anxiety - works remarkably well.</p>`,
