      heading: "Does porn cause erectile dysfunction?",
      body: `<p><strong>This is one of the most-asked and most-contested questions in young-men's sexual health, and the honest answer is: it's a real possibility for some men, but the science is genuinely unsettled, and "porn-induced ED" is not an established medical diagnosis.</strong> You deserve the real state of the evidence, not a confident slogan in either direction.</p><p>The hypothesis, laid out in an influential 2016 review in <em>Behavioral Sciences</em> (Park and colleagues), is that frequent, high-novelty internet pornography use can condition a man's arousal toward the screen in a way that makes real-partner sex feel comparatively under-stimulating - so arousal and erections decline with a partner. The review gathered clinical reports and argued that, for some men, stopping porn reversed the problem. It called for controlled studies to test it.</p><p>The counter-case is substantial. A 2015 study by Prause and colleagues using brain measurements argued that viewing sexual stimuli was associated with <em>greater</em> sexual responsiveness, not dysfunction. A 2019 integrative review of the observational studies concluded there was little solid evidence that pornography use induces erectile dysfunction, while acknowledging that proper longitudinal studies were lacking. Critics point out that the pro-hypothesis evidence is largely correlational and anecdotal, with no controlled trials, and that men who report both heavy porn use and ED may share an underlying factor (like anxiety) that drives both. The next section covers what this means for you practically.</p>`,
