      heading: "Do anabolic steroids cause ED?",
      body: `<p><strong>Yes - anabolic-androgenic steroids are a well-established cause of erectile dysfunction and low testosterone in young men, especially after a cycle, and the effects can take months to resolve or sometimes persist.</strong> With performance-enhancing and physique drug use common among young, gym-focused men, this is a frequent hidden cause.</p><p>The mechanism is clear endocrinology. When a man takes exogenous anabolic steroids (synthetic testosterone or derivatives), his brain senses high androgen levels and <strong>shuts down his own natural testosterone production</strong> - the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis powers down, and the testicles often shrink. While "on," erections may be fine or even enhanced; the problem typically hits <strong>after stopping (post-cycle)</strong>, when the body's own testosterone has been suppressed and hasn't yet recovered. The result is a crash into low testosterone, with low libido, fatigue and erectile dysfunction. Recovery can take months, and with heavy or prolonged use it can be incomplete.</p><p>This also applies to some "natural testosterone boosters," SARMs, and other hormonally active supplements bought online, whose contents and effects are often poorly characterized. If a young man has ED along with a history of steroid or SARM use, that history is central - and it's exactly the kind of thing to disclose honestly to a clinician, who can check hormone levels and discuss recovery. The uncomfortable truth is that chasing muscle with androgens can cost erections; the good news is that medical support and time usually help the axis recover.</p>`,
