      heading: "When should a young man see a doctor about ED?",
      body: `<p><strong>If erectile difficulty is persistent (more than a few weeks of a consistent pattern), causing you distress, or accompanied by other symptoms, it's worth seeing a clinician - and doing so sooner rather than later is the smart move, not an overreaction.</strong> Many young men delay out of embarrassment; that delay is the real mistake.</p><p>Clear reasons to get evaluated:</p><ul><li>A <strong>persistent pattern</strong> rather than the occasional off night.</li><li><strong>Loss of morning and spontaneous erections</strong>, which points more toward a physical cause.</li><li>ED alongside <strong>other symptoms</strong> - low libido, fatigue, mood changes, or signs of low testosterone.</li><li>You have <strong>risk factors</strong> - weight gain, high blood pressure, diabetes or a strong family history.</li><li>Your ED <strong>started after a new medication</strong> (an SSRI, finasteride) or substance.</li><li>It's causing you significant <strong>distress or relationship strain</strong> - that alone is reason enough.</li></ul><p>Two reassurances. First, this is routine for clinicians - they have these conversations constantly, and modern telehealth makes it private and low-friction (no waiting-room awkwardness). Second, getting checked usually brings good news: either reassurance that you're healthy and the cause is manageable, or the early catch of something far easier to treat now than later. The worst option is suffering silently for months or years while an easily-addressed cause goes unexamined. ED is a normal medical issue, not a character flaw - treat it like any other symptom worth sorting out.</p>`,
